  "worked_solution": "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}=3{b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $b$'s in terms of $c$'s. From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n{b}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}={b}_{1}+6(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1})\\,\u27f9\\,{b}_{1}={c}_{2}-2{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nSince $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$, we can write the vector $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5{b}_{1}+2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5({c}_{2}-2{c}_{1})+2(\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{1}) \\\\  & =-5{c}_{2}+10{c}_{1}+\\frac{2}{3}{c}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{3}{c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{32}{3} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$",
